  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Subarctic Darner (Aeshna subarctica) is a medium-sized dragonfly belonging to the Darner family, characterized by its slender body and swift aerial hunting abilities. This species is adapted to cooler climates and is found throughout New England, particularly in northern regions.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Thoracic Stripes",
      "description": "Blue or green stripes running along the thorax (middle body section) are the most distinctive feature",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Abdominal Markings",
      "description": "The abdomen displays a series of blue or green spots and rings on a dark background",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Size",
      "description": "Medium-sized darner, typically 2.5-3 inches long with a relatively narrow body compared to other dragonflies",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Subarctic Darners prefer cool, acidic lakes, ponds, and boggy wetlands with emergent vegetation, particularly in forested areas of northern New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Like all darners, Aeshna subarctica are aerial acrobats that can fly in any direction, including backwards, and are voracious predators that consume hundreds of small insects daily.",
